+### Correction (2026-11-20, activation 44647221858) — momentum/reversal signal was degenerate
+
+- **Defect:** `regime_stability.momentum_signals` and `mean_reversion_signals` computed `np.mean(np.log(closes[i-lookback+1:i+1]))` — the mean log *price level* over the window, not the log *return*. For any price series above 1.0 that quantity is positive, so momentum emitted a constant `+1` (always long) and reversal a constant `-1` (always short), independent of returns. On the collected universe the legacy formula produced a single constant weight on 27/30 (ticker, lookback) cells and a constant `+1.0` on 9/10 tickers at every lookback (the three exceptions are NVDA, whose split-adjusted price crosses $1).
+- **Consequence:** the recorded `momentum_results.json` medians are exactly reproducible by a constant-long (buy-and-hold) backtest — 10/10 assets MATCH (e.g. AAPL [0.071, 0.054, 0.092, 0.069] = the segment buy-and-hold log returns). The admitted "REGIME_STABLE positive edge in 7/10" was therefore a statement that 7 large-cap series rose over 2009-2026, not that a momentum signal works. The same defect explains the previously "surprising" corroborations that were recorded as evidence of a genuine anomaly: reversal-vs-momentum P&L correlation -0.99 (constant short vs constant long), "horizon-stable in magnitude" at lookback 20/60 (identical constant position), and "lookback-robust across 3/5/10 with 0 lookback-sensitive cases" (identical constant position at every lookback).
+- **Status change:** the momentum, longer-horizon momentum, momentum lookback-sweep and mean-reversion frontier cells are **RETRACTED / INVALID, pending re-derivation** from the repaired signal. Their artifacts are preserved unchanged as historical evidence of the defective run; they must not be cited as evidence about momentum or reversal.
+- **Why verification did not catch it:** every independent verifier re-implemented the same formula (`np.mean(np.log(closes[...]))` appears in `verify_momentum.py`, `verify_momentum_lookback_sweep.py`, `verify_momentum_longer_horizon.py`, `verify_mean_reversion.py`, `momentum_horizon_gap_analysis.py`), so independent recomputation certified the artifact while sharing the mis-specification. Common-mode defect, not a computation error. `cross_sectional_momentum.py` uses `closes[i]/closes[i-lookback]-1` and is unaffected; `breakout_signals` uses a correct threshold comparison and is unaffected.
+- **Repair:** both signals now use `log(closes[i]) - log(closes[i-lookback])`. Added `tests/test_regime_stability.py::TestMomentumSignalContract` (7 tests: both signs present, reversal == -momentum, sign follows trailing return, sub-$1 prices do not force a short, monotone series is long, neutral before lookback, prefix invariance) — the gate whose absence allowed the defect to reach the evidence base.
+- **Cost-sensitivity result (task R-001, this activation):** with the repaired signal the predeclared cost grid (0x / 1x repo realistic / 2x / 4x, lookbacks 3/5/10, matched coin-flip null, 10 tickers) finds **no positive REGIME_STABLE edge at zero cost on any asset** (0/10 assets with >=2 edge lookbacks, 0/30 EDGE cells), so the cost question is moot and the universe verdict is `NO_BASELINE_EDGE`, not `COST_FRAGILE`. Cost drag at 1x is 0.16-3.27%/yr for 8/10 assets with 92-109x/yr turnover; NVDA's costed cells are not interpretable because 9/136 folds (1x) and 7/120 (TSLA) have equity wipeouts whose log1p clip makes the median depend on the surviving subsample. Artifact `state/check_artifacts/momentum_cost_sensitivity_results.json`; independently verified 120/120 cells + 10/10 legacy cross-consistency.
+- **Process delta:** add a mandatory signal-contract gate (non-degeneracy + sign-follows-input + reversal-is-negation + prefix invariance) before any new signal's results are admitted; a positive result from a constant-position signal is not evidence about the signal class.
